WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:11.840
Imagine an ecosystem made entirely of autonomous&nbsp;
machines. Where every ecological niche is filled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:18.800
by an artificial organism. In the video game&nbsp;
‘Horizon: Zero Dawn,’ a global calamity has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:24.480
led to a version of planet Earth inhabited&nbsp;
by animal-mimicking machines — from mighty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:31.280
herbivores to deadly predators. While these&nbsp;
synthetic creatures might not be ‘biological,’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:37.840
their fascinating interactions, unique behavioral&nbsp;
patterns, and hidden intricacies make them worthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:43.280
of study. The story takes place thousands&nbsp;
of years in the future, but a little thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.920
like the flow of time isn’t going to stop&nbsp;
this archivist from doing his fieldwork.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:54.960
So, for this entry into the archive, I’ll lead&nbsp;
us on another documentary style expedition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:00.320
investigating the artificial ‘biology’ of&nbsp;
these intriguing machines and revealing what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:06.560
animal roles they fill. We’ll need, though, to&nbsp;
stay alert—there are constant dangers in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:13.920
environment. Now, let’s leap forward in time,&nbsp;
and discover an ecosystem of metal and wire...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.400
Beginning in the safety of a region known as the&nbsp;
Embrace, we find a picturesque landscape that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:28.960
shows little signs of a planet-wide calamity.&nbsp;
Across much of the land, wounds of old have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:35.280
long since healed — although this reborn Earth is&nbsp;
much changed from the peak of human civilization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:41.120
Small populations of human hunter-gatherers&nbsp;
still inhabit this land — although most live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:47.120
within meager settlements, ever fearful of the&nbsp;
strange metal creatures that roam the woods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:51.760
whose function they have long forgotten.&nbsp;
Settling out into the wildness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:58.080
I find that some organic animal life does persist&nbsp;
in the shifting underbrush. But the roles they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:05.760
fill are small and infrequent, for this is no&nbsp;
longer their world. Peering out from the grass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:13.200
I spot a being roughly the size of a horse. Our&nbsp;
first machine. This is a Strider — a basic type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:19.280
of synthetic creature that fills the function of&nbsp;
an herbivore. Like organic herbivores, Striders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:26.320
spend much of their time quietly grazing in small&nbsp;
herds, moving from one patch of grass to another.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:32.800
Yet what use does a machine have for grass?&nbsp;
The answer lies in a small canister located&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:39.200
between the Strider’s haunches. This container&nbsp;
is filled with a green liquid called Blaze,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:45.120
a type of biofuel synthesized from plants that&nbsp;
is foundational to the artificial ecosystem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.880
of this world. The Striders are machines that&nbsp;
convert grass into fuel — much like how any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:57.760
living herbivore essentially uses vegetation to&nbsp;
power their bodies. Striders also bring blaze back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:04.480
to autonomous factories called Cauldrons, which&nbsp;
continue to repair and replace machines long after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:11.200
being forgotten. Yet this energy source makes&nbsp;
Striders a target for human hunter-gatherers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:17.360
This means that — like most herbivores — a&nbsp;
Strider’s life is one of constant vigilance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:24.800
When threat looms, their optical sensors turn an&nbsp;
uneasy orange. Skittish by nature, the Strider’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:31.200
first instinct when human hunters are near is to&nbsp;
run. And like horses, when the herd takes off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:37.600
they can move with breathtaking speed. With&nbsp;
all four legs pumping, they can easily outrun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:44.080
most dangers, only slowing when the coast is&nbsp;
clear. Not wanting to disturb these creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:51.120
I try to stay out of sight… …But that is easier&nbsp;
said than done. For guarding the outskirts of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:59.120
herd is a new type of machine. This is a Watcher,&nbsp;
and they make fieldwork a challenge. These small,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:05.840
bipedal beings spend their existence tirelessly&nbsp;
patrolling the perimeter of the Strider herd. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:12.240
precious little slips past their optical sensors&nbsp;
unnoticed. Animals serving as lookouts isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:18.560
unheard of in nature, with the designated sentry&nbsp;
a key role in meerkat society, for example. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:25.120
this sort of coordination between two species&nbsp;
(so to speak), is something unique to machines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:31.520
By warning the Striders of imminent dangers,&nbsp;
Watchers are almost like living security cameras.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:38.320
Well… living in a sense. And they also play&nbsp;
the role of bodyguard. With surprising speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:44.960
Watchers can launch themselves into a flying&nbsp;
kick. It’s an impressive, if unwieldy strategy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:50.800
Yet Watchers will fight to the very end while&nbsp;
the Strider herd flees, and unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:57.120
one has spotted me. Launching into their signature&nbsp;
kick, a Watcher misses my head, and suffers a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:04.400
pretty serious wipeout. Stumbling to their feet,&nbsp;
they seem rattled, but unharmed. Only their pride&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:11.200
is bruised. Not wanting to be insensitive,&nbsp;
I give my unsuccessful assailant the slip.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:17.200
Going forwards, I’ll have to be more careful, as&nbsp;
there are far more dangerous things than Watchers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:24.400
in this world of machines… To the north of the&nbsp;
Embrace, my path takes me to a region strewn with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:30.960
the scars of civilization. The crumbling remnants&nbsp;
of buildings have been all but swallowed up by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:38.720
steady encroachment of nature. Yet even after a&nbsp;
thousand years, a quiet melancholy still clings to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:46.560
this site. Yet there are machines in this broken,&nbsp;
unpredictable landscape. The deer-like Grazers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:53.040
are adaptable enough to brave this region. Native&nbsp;
to the rolling fields beyond the ruin’s outskirts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:57.760
Grazers possess a pair of metallic&nbsp;
antler-like structures that serve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:03.120
a curious function. On the ends of these&nbsp;
structures are a set of rotor blades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:09.840
which the Grazers use to shred grass and dirt&nbsp;
like sentient lawnmowers. With this unusual setup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:16.240
Grazers can consume plant matter faster than any&nbsp;
other machine. And like deer, where there is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:23.280
Grazer, there are invariably others. A herd of&nbsp;
grazers, all with their heads shoved in the dirt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:28.800
is an odd sight indeed. Yet Grazers don’t&nbsp;
have time to worry about their image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:34.960
as with four canisters of biofuel to fill,&nbsp;
they’re always on the move. But when a Grazer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:40.640
feels threatened, their antlers have another&nbsp;
function. When I move too close to one Grazer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:47.120
the machine takes a fearsome swipe at me. A&nbsp;
narrow miss. As lithe herbivore-analogues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:53.120
Grazers aren’t built for combat, and so this&nbsp;
unit scampers away once I stand my ground…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:59.920
Journeying deeper into the city ruins,&nbsp;
I find the feeling of desolation grows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:07.600
In this graveyard of humanity, I sense something&nbsp;
is lurking just out of sight. A terrifying machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:14.480
haunts this landscape. A Sawtooth. Rippling&nbsp;
with synthetic muscle, this cat-like hunter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:20.800
is the equivalent of an apex predator in the&nbsp;
machine ecosystem. Their metal jaws come equipped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:26.960
with a pair of curved, serrated spikes, which&nbsp;
resemble the formidable fangs of the largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:32.880
saber-toothed cats. There are rumors among the&nbsp;
local humans that the Cauldrons have been making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:39.760
larger and more dangerous machines in recent years&nbsp;
— and looking at the Sawtooth, I don’t doubt it...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:46.240
A machine this large might not seem capable of&nbsp;
great speed. Yet when a Sawtooth catches a target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:52.800
in the open, they can move with incredible&nbsp;
agility. Aside from their fangs, Sawtooths also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:59.280
possess claws powerful enough to shred through the&nbsp;
metal of these ruins. Trying to fight or run from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:06.240
a Sawtooth is futile. The only way I’ll live to&nbsp;
archive another day, is if I avoid the Sawtooth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:13.520
altogether. These ruins are their domain now, and&nbsp;
the time has come to make my exit. As the machines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:19.760
continue to nurture the ecosystem, nature will&nbsp;
eventually reclaim this region entirely, leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:26.640
behind no trace of the people who lived here. Even&nbsp;
monuments of metal and stone can’t last forever…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:35.360
Venturing to the west, I find a vast scrubland,&nbsp;
baking in the sweltering rays of the rising sun.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:39.520
Yet machines have conquered this&nbsp;
unforgiving environment as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:46.720
with unique adaptations to brave the arid region.&nbsp;
These are Longlegs — machines with a tall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:53.360
bird-like appearance. Like the ostriches of today,&nbsp;
Longlegs are stuck on the ground, unable to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:59.360
flight. Yet through my observations, I’ve found&nbsp;
that Longlegs have a hidden ability. Mounted on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:06.560
their chest are two bulbous sacs that resemble&nbsp;
external lungs. These artificial sacs are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:14.080
in fact, sonic amplifiers. When a Longleg feels&nbsp;
threatened, they can unleash a booming, concussive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:20.800
soundwave so powerful it sends particulates&nbsp;
flying. For this reason, I’d recommend wearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:27.680
serious ear protection when observing Longlegs up&nbsp;
close. Aside from their soundwaves, Longlegs are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:34.960
also capable of alarming bursts of speed. They’ll&nbsp;
even leap into astonishing flying kicks, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:42.400
look both beautiful… and frankly absurd. All in&nbsp;
all, these unusual machines are better left alone…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:50.960
My intuition takes me northwards, where I&nbsp;
find a ruined landscape of twisted metal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:58.480
This rusted necropolis is a vast scrapyard –&nbsp;
the skeletal remnants of human civilization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:04.800
And a unique kind of machine has come to pick&nbsp;
these metal bones clean. Prowling in the shadows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:12.160
of the junkyard are packs of Scrappers. Small but&nbsp;
deadly, Scrappers rule over this ruined kingdom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:18.320
With their humped posture and clawed feet,&nbsp;
Scrappers resemble the skulking forms of hyenas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:24.880
And like hyenas, Scrappers can be uncommonly&nbsp;
vicious. Yet there is another side to these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:32.160
seemingly simple terrors. Scrapper jaws are&nbsp;
composed of four unique circular grinders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:38.640
But these jaws aren’t just for hunting. True to&nbsp;
their name, Scrappers use their curious mouths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:45.040
to recycle metal resources. In grinding down&nbsp;
salvage piles and the bodies of inactive machines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:52.160
the Scrappers gather materials that can be used in&nbsp;
the construction of more synthetic life. And so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:57.440
these machines fill a role similar to&nbsp;
scavengers in this ecosystem of metal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:03.520
and improve the health of the environment. Yet&nbsp;
Scrappers aren’t purely altruistic. If were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:09.920
to be spotted by one, they would waste no time&nbsp;
in trying to recycle me. For any non-machines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:18.640
this metal cemetery is a treacherous place...&nbsp;
Amid the dry badlands, the flow of rivers creates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:25.200
isolated pockets of vegetation. And patrolling&nbsp;
rare wetlands are one-of-a-kind machines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:33.200
Shell-Walkers. These scuttling oddities move&nbsp;
on six stout legs, and can brave both wet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:39.600
and dry terrain. And unlike all other types of&nbsp;
machines I’ve observed so far, Shell-Walkers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:46.480
double as transport vehicles — a class dedicated&nbsp;
to ferrying materials back to Cauldrons. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:53.120
carry their quarry in hexagonal cargo containers&nbsp;
on their back, which they swap regularly. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:58.480
their ever-changing shells, a Shell-Walker’s&nbsp;
lifestyle mirrors that of a hermit crab.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:05.280
If you ask me, a futuristic robot converging&nbsp;
on a crab makes complete sense, as crabs are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:11.520
the pinnacle of evolution. And Shell-Walkers will&nbsp;
defend their cargo to the end. On their frontside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:18.160
Shell-Walkers possess two powerful arms — one&nbsp;
tipped with a formidable claw… and the other with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:25.280
a mysterious, circular disc. Upon moving closer,&nbsp;
I discover this is some form of energy shield&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:30.400
generator — that the Shell-Walkers can deploy to&nbsp;
protect their left side while they attack with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:36.080
their right. While energy shields are certainly&nbsp;
unique to machines, this sort of asymmetrical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:42.480
defensive setup can also be found in the natural&nbsp;
world. Fiddler crabs are armed with one claw that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:48.880
is significantly larger than the other, which they&nbsp;
use to fight over burrows. With their versatile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:54.560
crab-like body, Shell-Walkers are well&nbsp;
equipped to traverse this unpredictable world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.320 --> 00:13:01.440
Speaking of which, as I venture into the&nbsp;
western wastes, the climate becomes even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:07.520
harsher. Rusting under the afternoon sun&nbsp;
are the remains of a great satellite array.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:12.560
Before the world fell to ruin, these&nbsp;
dishes might have been for communication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:20.000
hurtling signals out to the stars. Yet now, these&nbsp;
ruins send no messages save a silent reminder that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:26.560
nothing lasts forever. And seemingly on cue,&nbsp;
circling overhead are what appears to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:33.200
flock of vultures — a universal omen of grim&nbsp;
tidings. Yet like most creatures in this land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:37.680
these foragers are made of metal.&nbsp;
I’ve entered into the territory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:44.880
of Glinthawks. These aerial machines keep a grim&nbsp;
vigil over this stretch of the badlands. Their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:50.720
exceptionally keen eyesight means that little&nbsp;
slips through this region without their notice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:57.520
Yet despite their fearsome appearance, Glinthawks&nbsp;
play an important role in the machine biosphere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.520 --> 00:14:04.000
Wherever the carcass of non-functional machines&nbsp;
can be found, a Glinthawk is sure to be nearby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:10.480
breaking down the metal for resources in a manner&nbsp;
similar to a Scrapper. Like modern vultures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:17.760
Glinthawks are obligate scavengers, breaking down&nbsp;
the parts others leave behind. It’s an unsettling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:24.160
profession, but someone has to do it. As long as&nbsp;
the flock is distracted with their salvage duties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:30.320
I should be safe to pass through this area if&nbsp;
I do so cautiously. Yet one Glinthawk seems to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:37.120
have grown wise to my presence. In a surprising&nbsp;
turn, the metal fiend sends volley after volley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:43.520
of freezing liquid my way. This is actually the&nbsp;
Glinthawk’s coolant, which they can jettison from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:49.440
their bodies as a kind of chemical defense.&nbsp;
Fleeing across the desert, I find that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:54.460
Glinthawk, thankfully, doesn’t seem interested&nbsp;
in an extended chase. Why bother with a moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.460 --> 00:14:55.920
target, when there are so many stationary ones&nbsp;
strewn among the sands of this perilous expanse?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.920 --> 00:15:02.720
If I don’t wish to be claimed by this land myself,&nbsp;
I need to find a way to escape this heat. A cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:09.600
dip in the water seems promising… except this&nbsp;
river is occupied. Swimming just under the surface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:16.000
is a huge Snapmaw. These imposing machines are&nbsp;
a force to be reckoned with both in the water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:22.560
and on land. Everything from their gait, to their&nbsp;
anatomy, to their general behavior closely mirrors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:28.640
that of crocodiles. Yet if the jaws of crocodiles&nbsp;
weren’t frightening enough, the lower mandible of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:34.880
the Snapmaw is actually a chainsaw-like blade&nbsp;
with a system of small grinders at its base.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:42.320
If one strays too close to the water’s edge, it’s&nbsp;
over in an instant. Keeping my distance from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:48.960
riverbank, however, I notice Snapmaws actually&nbsp;
spend most of their time basking in the sun’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:55.680
rays. Like many so-called ‘cold-blooded’ animals,&nbsp;
crocodiles sun themselves to increase their body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.680 --> 00:16:02.320
temperature. Yet what reason might a machine have&nbsp;
for regular sunbathing? The answer lies in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:09.520
rows of solar panels lining the Snapmaws back.&nbsp;
This clever energy-gathering array means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.520 --> 00:16:15.440
even when a Snapmaw seems to be inactive, they&nbsp;
are actually gaining the power needed for their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:22.560
daily movements. In this land, even a machine&nbsp;
as menacing as a Snapmaw has hidden intricacies…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:31.760
With a swim seeming unwise, I escape the heat&nbsp;
with a change in elevation. Throughout the waste,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.760 --> 00:16:36.080
isolated plateaus rise so high&nbsp;
their peaks are crowned with snow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:42.400
In these frigid microclimates, the strange&nbsp;
landscapes are home to even stranger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:49.600
machines. Trudging through the snow is a bizarre&nbsp;
procession of Freeze Bellowbacks. These hardy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:56.880
slow-moving machines are well acclimated to the&nbsp;
world of ice and snow. With their sloped posture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.880 --> 00:17:02.640
Bellowbacks subtly echo the body plan&nbsp;
of therapod dinosaurs like Baryonyx.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:09.040
As another type of transport machine, Freeze&nbsp;
Bellowbacks carry large reserves of coolant in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:15.760
hefty cargo sacs on their back, and four smaller&nbsp;
sacs under their neck. When perturbed, Freeze&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:22.160
Bellowbacks will spray a high-pressure stream of&nbsp;
coolant from their nozzle. It’s a devastating and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:28.480
effective defense mechanism, even if it looks…&nbsp;
a bit like sneezing. In these low temperatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:34.800
a blast of coolant can be deadly. I think it’s&nbsp;
best to let the Bellowback procession pass on by…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:42.720
Hiking down to the base of the snowy mesa, I&nbsp;
am drawn to the sound of thundering footsteps.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.360 --> 00:17:49.760
Creeping closer, I stumble into the midst&nbsp;
of a herd of giants. These are Behemoths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:54.800
beasts that look like they could carry the weight&nbsp;
of a mountain — or smash their way through one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.800 --> 00:18:01.360
Like the largest rhinos or elephants, Behemoths&nbsp;
are the megafauna of the environment of machines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.360 --> 00:18:07.040
The mightiest of the transport class, their&nbsp;
belly has a large space into which a circular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.040 --> 00:18:13.520
cargo container is locked in place by clamps.&nbsp;
And like all transport machines, Behemoths are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:20.160
fiercely protective when it comes to their cargo.&nbsp;
In awe, I watch from a distance as a Behemoth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:26.640
levitates a ring of boulders using anti-gravity&nbsp;
technology, then slams them down with a force so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:33.520
great it shakes the valley. This mysterious power,&nbsp;
made possible through a forgotten technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:39.520
makes the Behemoths an insurmountable force.&nbsp;
While they typically use this power to gather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.520 --> 00:18:45.440
minerals and other materials for transport,&nbsp;
if I’m spotted, these giants won’t hesitate to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.440 --> 00:18:52.240
unleash their crushing force to end my expedition&nbsp;
prematurely. Worrying as such an outcome might be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:56.560
I can’t help but stay transfixed&nbsp;
by the strength of these beings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.560 --> 00:19:02.720
In time, however, the convoy lumbers on,&nbsp;
continuing their steady march across the valley…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:10.240
Travelling as far west as I dare venture, I reach&nbsp;
a region where the greatest terror of the machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:17.520
world dwells. No amount of prior observation&nbsp;
prepares me for the sight that is the Thunderjaw.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:23.840
Bristling with advanced weaponry, from tip to&nbsp;
tail, the Thunderjaw is a bringer of pure dread.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.640 --> 00:19:29.840
In terms of size and sheer might, they&nbsp;
represent a strength not seen in this region&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:36.640
since the Tyrannosaurus Rex some ninety-million&nbsp;
years ago. And unlike most machines, this metal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:43.200
super-predator’s sole function is combat. From&nbsp;
their jaws, an array of cannons bring destruction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:49.520
like the breath of a dragon. On their hips are a&nbsp;
pair of disk launchers, which rain down fire and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:56.080
smoke from above. Even without their futuristic&nbsp;
weaponry, a Thunderjaw is still more than capable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.080 --> 00:20:01.680
of dispatching any threat, with their massive&nbsp;
tail perhaps their greatest weapon of all. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:08.320
further I stay from this machine, the better. Yet&nbsp;
in the glow of the setting sun, even a Thunderjaw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:14.800
can look majestic. Without a visible target,&nbsp;
Thunderjaws behave like most any animal, plodding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:21.280
about their environment at a leisurely pace. While&nbsp;
I watch the Thunderjaw pass on, I can’t help but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.280 --> 00:20:27.360
wonder what this land might be like if machines&nbsp;
and humans weren’t so diametrically opposed...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:34.000
But my journey is not over yet.&nbsp;
At the edge of a shimmering lake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:42.160
footsteps echo over the water — so loud the entire&nbsp;
land seems to tremble in anticipation. Overhead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:49.200
the largest machine I’ve ever seen appears to&nbsp;
eclipse the very sun. This moving landmark is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.200 --> 00:20:55.360
Tallneck. They can be found across many different&nbsp;
environments, although their numbers are scarce.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.920 --> 00:21:02.480
Unlike every other machine I’ve documented today,&nbsp;
these beings are in no way distrustful of humans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:07.200
With their silhouette and gait, Tallnecks&nbsp;
seem somewhere between a Giraffe and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:13.920
largest sauropod dinosaurs. The strange disk that&nbsp;
functions like the Tallneck’s head serves as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:20.160
communications array, sending information&nbsp;
to and from other machines. In this way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:26.800
the solitary Tallnecks act as the unofficial&nbsp;
hubs of the machine ecosystems. It’s a vital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.800 --> 00:21:33.360
yet seemingly lonely purpose, as the Tallnecks&nbsp;
spend their existence walking in an eternal loop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:39.120
never thanked for their vital contribution to&nbsp;
the machine biosphere. Perhaps the Tallnecks are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:45.680
oblivious to their surroundings, or perhaps they&nbsp;
get lonesome on their isolated march. Though I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.680 --> 00:21:51.280
well aware I’m likely projecting my own emotions&nbsp;
onto the Tallnecks, I decide to stay with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.280 --> 00:21:57.840
beasts as long as I am able. After all, a path can&nbsp;
be a little less lonely when walked with a friend…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:07.360
There’s much we’ve uncovered about this world of&nbsp;
machines, yet many key mysteries remain unsolved.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:13.280
From the dense jungles of the south, to the frozen&nbsp;
wilds of the far north, there are a whole host of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.280 --> 00:22:18.960
machines I’ve yet to document, and secrets I’ve&nbsp;
yet to uncover. We’ll venture further across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:25.280
the world of Horizon: Zero Dawn in Part Two,&nbsp;
exploring the more bizarre and more dangerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.280 --> 00:22:33.280
machines in the lost reaches of the world. But&nbsp;
for now, our journey is at an end… As always,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.280 --> 00:22:38.720
thanks for watching. If you enjoyed this entry,&nbsp;
please lend your support and like, subscribe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:53.840
and hit the notification icon to stay up to date&nbsp;
on all things Curious. See you in the next video.

